Use a nondecreasing stack of indices; a shorter bar closes rectangles for taller stacked bars. A final virtual zero-height bar processes remaining positive heights. Time: O(n); extra space: O(n).

```javascript
function solve(heights) {
  const stack = [];
  let best = 0;
  const n = heights.length;

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    const current = i === n ? 0 : heights[i];

    while (
      stack.length > 0 &&
      heights[stack[stack.length - 1]] > current
    ) {
      const height = heights[stack.pop()];
      const left = stack.length > 0
        ? stack[stack.length - 1]
        : -1;
      const width = i - left - 1;
      best = Math.max(best, height * width);
    }

    if (i < n) stack.push(i);
  }

  return best;
}
```

Call solve(heights) directly with nonnegative, unit-width bar heights. No stdin/stdout, imports, or exports; the input is not modified, and an empty array returns 0.

