The preceding implementation works for ordinary integers, but the contract gives no numeric bounds: JavaScript rounding can make current + 1 equal current, causing an infinite loop. Add exact-step guards without changing the contract.

Manual checks:
- []: the Set is empty; returns 0.
- [7, 7]: the Set contains only 7; neither neighbor exists, so returns 1.
- [3, -1, 2, 0, 2, 1, 8]: 3 and 2 have predecessors and are skipped. Starting at -1 counts -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, then stops because 4 is absent; 0 and 1 are skipped. The run at 8 has length 1; returns 5.
- [9007199254740992]: its predecessor is absent, but adding 1 rounds back to the same value. The old loop never terminates; the corrected exact-step check stops and returns 1.

```javascript
function solve(nums) {
  const values = new Set(nums);
  let longest = 0;

  for (const value of values) {
    const previous = value - 1;
    if (value - previous === 1 && values.has(previous)) {
      continue;
    }

    let current = value;
    let length = 1;

    while (true) {
      const next = current + 1;
      if (next - current !== 1 || !values.has(next)) {
        break;
      }
      current = next;
      length++;
    }

    longest = Math.max(longest, length);
  }

  return longest;
}
```

